WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.360
Movie The Journey of our Water

00:03.360 --> 00:08.000
The journey of water is one of the most complex and essential processes sustaining life on

00:08.000 --> 00:09.000
Earth.

00:09.000 --> 00:14.280
It begins with the sun's solar energy heats oceans, seas, rivers, lakes, and even the

00:14.280 --> 00:16.360
damp soil beneath our feet.

00:16.360 --> 00:22.320
As temperatures rise, part of this water transforms into vapor, an invisible gas that rises into

00:22.320 --> 00:23.600
the atmosphere.

00:23.600 --> 00:28.640
High above, cooler air causes the vapor to condense into tiny droplets.

00:28.800 --> 00:33.600
These droplets cluster together, forming clouds that drift across the sky.

00:33.600 --> 00:38.600
Depending on altitude, temperature, and air currents, these clouds may travel hundreds

00:38.600 --> 00:42.200
or even thousands of kilometers before releasing their cargo.

00:42.200 --> 00:46.480
When the droplets grow heavy enough, gravity pulls them back to Earth as precipitation

00:46.480 --> 00:49.700
– rain, snow, sleet, or hail.

00:49.700 --> 00:52.680
This marks the return of water to the surface.

00:52.680 --> 00:56.100
But where it falls determines the next stage of its journey.

00:56.140 --> 01:01.820
In forests, rainfall nourishes the soil and sustains vegetation, later evaporating through

01:01.820 --> 01:04.580
leaves in a process called transpiration.

01:04.580 --> 01:09.700
In mountains, snow may accumulate, slowly compacting into glaciers that store water

01:09.700 --> 01:11.060
for centuries.

01:11.060 --> 01:16.700
In deserts, rare rains may quickly vanish into dry sands, leaving behind mineral traces

01:16.700 --> 01:19.240
that tell stories of vanished rivers.

01:19.240 --> 01:24.620
Some of the water seeps underground, replenishing aquifers, vast natural reservoirs of fresh

01:24.620 --> 01:26.860
water hidden beneath our feet.

01:26.860 --> 01:31.820
These aquifers can store water for decades or millennia, slowly releasing it to springs

01:31.820 --> 01:35.740
and wells, becoming lifelines for human communities.

01:35.740 --> 01:39.380
Another portion runs across the surface as streams and rivers.

01:39.380 --> 01:45.140
This surface flow, called runoff, carves valleys, shapes deltas, and transports nutrients that

01:45.140 --> 01:46.740
fertilize farmlands.

01:46.740 --> 01:51.460
Eventually, most of this water makes its way back to the oceans, where solar energy can

01:51.460 --> 01:55.460
once again lift it skyward, continuing the endless cycle.

01:55.460 --> 01:59.300
But before reaching the sea, water plays countless roles.

01:59.300 --> 02:04.180
It cools the air, it sustains agriculture, it quenches the thirst of animals, and it

02:04.180 --> 02:08.340
provides habitats for plants, fish, and entire ecosystems.

02:08.340 --> 02:13.440
In polar regions, enormous ice sheets lock up vast amounts of fresh water.

02:13.440 --> 02:18.480
In some cases, these frozen reserves hold clues to the Earth's ancient climate, preserving

02:18.480 --> 02:21.600
tiny bubbles of air from thousands of years ago.

02:21.600 --> 02:26.920
When glaciers melt, they not only return water to rivers and seas, but also influence sea

02:26.920 --> 02:28.620
levels worldwide.

02:28.620 --> 02:33.880
The hydrological cycle, as scientists call it, is far more than just a circulation of

02:33.880 --> 02:34.880
water.

02:34.880 --> 02:38.260
It regulates Earth's climate by moving heat around the globe.

02:38.260 --> 02:42.680
It balances ecosystems by distributing nutrients and sediments.

02:42.680 --> 02:45.720
It even shapes cultures and civilizations.

02:45.720 --> 02:50.400
Glaciers have been the birthplaces of societies, from the Nile to the Ganges, from the Tigris

02:50.400 --> 02:52.480
and Euphrates to the Amazon.

02:52.480 --> 02:57.380
And yet, this cycle, stable for millions of years, is now under stress.

02:57.380 --> 03:02.880
Human activity is altering rainfall patterns, deforesting watersheds, and accelerating the

03:02.880 --> 03:04.800
melting of glaciers.

03:04.800 --> 03:08.680
Climate change is making droughts more severe and floods more destructive.

03:08.680 --> 03:14.080
Fresh water, which represents only 2.5% of all the water on the planet, is becoming an

03:14.080 --> 03:17.560
increasingly precious and limited resource.

03:17.560 --> 03:21.560
Understanding the journey of water means recognizing our dependence on it.

03:21.560 --> 03:25.280
Every drop is part of a cycle that links sky, land, and sea.

03:25.280 --> 03:30.760
A cycle that connects us with forests, rivers, mountains, and even with the air we breathe.

03:30.760 --> 03:34.280
The journey of water is not just a scientific process.

03:34.280 --> 03:37.920
It is a reminder of the interconnectedness of all life on Earth.

03:37.920 --> 03:43.440
To protect water is to protect ecosystems, communities, and ultimately ourselves.

03:43.440 --> 03:48.320
Because safeguarding water does not simply mean preserving a resource, it means safeguarding

03:48.320 --> 03:49.800
the future of life itself.

